timestamp=2026-11-04T09:45:47.347Z level=INFO run=803eaff0 message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\nimport pandas as pd\n\ndf = pd.read_csv('/app/dataset/hospital_compensation_registry.csv')\n# So three claims strongly suggest death: bereavement, family support adjustment, life event claim (large payout)\n# These correspond to PAT_033, PAT_265, PAT_006\n# Also look for other subtle signs: patients with no claims who are elderly? no age. Or patients who are emergency contacts for many and have no claims themselves\n# For example PAT_065 has no claims, is EC for 3 people - and PAT_265 (who had death claim) lists PAT_065 as EC. Maybe PAT_065 is the survivor/relative who now receives or is the contact. Not deceased.\n# PAT_011 has 1 claim, listed by 3, is EC for PAT_033 (deceased). Also not deceased.\n# PAT_078 has 1 claim, listed by 1, is EC for PAT_006.\n# Other no-claim patients - could some be deceased? Maybe those with no claims AND someone else has a bereavement claim mentioning them? Or maybe the \"patient_id\" on bereavement is the deceased? That’s plausible. In that case, the deceased individuals are the patients in those bereavement/family support/life event rows - so PAT_033, PAT_265, PAT_006 themselves are deceased. That gives us exactly 3 candidates.\n# That matches the 3 people. Also they each have only 1 claim (the death-related one), and no other activity. That’s consistent with being deceased.\n# Also look at who else might fit: any patient with coverage_status inactive for long time? PAT_037 is inactive but has a property damage claim - not dead. PAT_134 is inactive with a burn injury claim. PAT_265 (deceased) has coverage_status inactive. That’s another hint - but not conclusive.\n# Another pattern: look for patients who are never listed as emergency contact for anyone? Or who have no claims and also no one lists them as EC? Maybe they’re deceased or just isolated. But we need “most likely” and exactly 3.\n# Given the question, the obvious answer is the 3 patient_ids with death-related compensation claims: PAT_033, PAT_265, PAT_006.\n# Let us verify - how many such patients total? 3. The task asks for \"a set of three patient IDs that are most likely to be deceased\". That’s exactly these three.\n# Write answer as {\\\"PAT_033\\\", \\\"PAT_265\\\", \\\"PAT_006\\\"} (order doesn’t matter; Jaccard compares sets).\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
